# context prints the format, layers, registry, and rules for an agent.
cd $EXAMPLES/refund
exits 0 specval context
cmp stdout $WORK/refund.txt
! stderr .

# With an empty registry, context says so.
cd $WORK/empty
exits 0 specval context
stdout '^## Registered services\n\(none yet\)$'

# Without a .specval.yaml, context exits 2.
cd $WORK
exits 2 specval context
stderr '^specval: no .specval.yaml found'

-- empty/.specval.yaml --
-- refund.txt --
# Spec rules (specval)

Every change needs a passing spec at specs/<id>.yaml before code is written. Scaffold: `specval new <id>`. Validate: `specval check <file>`. Each finding includes a fix.

## Format
```yaml
specval: 1
id: ORD-142                # ticket id; file is specs/<id>.yaml
kind: feature              # feature | bugfix | chore
title: Customer can request a refund
summary: One paragraph - the problem and the intended behavior.
acceptance:                # testable criteria, ids AC1, AC2, ...
  - { id: AC1, text: "Given a delivered order, when the customer requests a refund, then ..." }
services:
  new:                     # services this change introduces
    - { name: RefundEngine, layer: engine, purpose: Computes refund amounts }
  changed: [OrderManager]  # registered services whose behavior changes
calls:                     # every service-to-service call the change adds or alters
  - { from: OrderManager, to: RefundEngine, op: CalculateRefund, covers: [AC1] }
out_of_scope: [Partial refunds]
```

## Layers (caller -> may call)
- manager -> engine, accessor
- engine -> accessor
- accessor -> resource
- resource -> nothing
Service names end with their layer: Manager, Engine, Accessor, Resource.

## Registered services
- manager: OrderManager
- engine: PricingEngine
- accessor: CatalogAccessor, OrderAccessor

## Rules
- ORD001 (error) Only OrderManager calls PricingEngine: Pricing is orchestrated in one place so discounts are applied consistently.
- SV001 (error) Spec matches the specval format: Required fields, field names, and value types follow the schema (`specval schema`). Other rules run only once this passes.
- SV101 (error) Calls use known services: Each call's `from` and `to` is a registered service or is declared in `services.new`.
- SV102 (error) Calls follow the layer rules: A service may call only the layers its own layer allows (see Layers).
- SV103 (error) New services are named for their layer: A new service's name ends with its layer's suffix (Manager, Engine, Accessor, Resource).
- SV104 (error) New services are actually new: A service already in the registry belongs under `services.changed`, not `services.new`.
- SV105 (error) New services use a known layer: A new service's `layer` is one of the DPL layers.
- SV106 (error) New services are used: Every service in `services.new` appears in at least one call.
- SV107 (error) Every acceptance criterion is implemented by a call: Each acceptance criterion id appears in the `covers` list of at least one call, so every requirement traces to the services that implement it.
- SV108 (error) Calls cover real acceptance criteria: Every id in a call's `covers` is defined under `acceptance`.
- SV109 (error) Changed services are registered: Every name in `services.changed` is in the registry.
- SV201 (warning) Changed services take part in a call: A service listed in `services.changed` should appear in at least one call.
